---
/*
  The price tiers for a service, rendered from the Services collection.

  WHY THIS EXISTS

  Four services have a hand-built page. Fieldwork has its prices typed into its
  own page file. The remaining three render from src/pages/services/[slug].astro,
  which had nowhere to read a price from at all, so AI Automation and Digital
  Marketing showed none.

  That is not a small omission. Two of the seven things Quadem sells had no
  number anywhere on their page, while the homepage next door showed cards from
  $3,000. Found 2 September 2026 by comparing the two side by side.

  THE CARDS THEMSELVES ARE NOT DRAWN HERE ANY MORE

  This file used to carry its own card design: smaller than every other page's,
  left aligned where the rest are centred, a "Most chosen" badge where the rest
  say "Most Popular", and no button at all, so the two newest services quoted a
  price and gave nobody a way to act on it.

  It now resolves the headings and hands the tiers to PricingCards, which is the
  one card design the whole site uses. Those two services gained a working
  button by doing nothing but this.

  RENDERS NOTHING WHEN THERE ARE NO TIERS

  A service with an empty pricingSection gets no section, not an empty one. That
  keeps this safe on the shared service template before every service has
  prices, and it means the CMS is the only thing that decides.
*/
import PricingCards from './PricingCards.astro';

interface Props {
    pricing?: any;
    serviceTitle?: string;
}

const { pricing, serviceTitle } = Astro.props;

const tiers = Array.isArray(pricing?.plans) ? pricing.plans : [];
const heading = pricing?.heading || (serviceTitle ? `${serviceTitle} pricing` : 'Pricing');
---

<PricingCards
    tiers={tiers}
    heading={heading}
    subtitle={pricing?.subtitle}
    note={pricing?.note}
    sectionId="pricing"
/>
